    "title": "Rain couldn’t stop this freshman from stealing the show at Ohio State football’s spring game",
    "text": "COLUMBUS, Ohio — A rainy afternoon inside Ohio Stadium didn’t get in the way of Ohio State’s annual spring game. Instead, it provided a stereotypical backdrop for Big Ten football, giving an Ohio State roster with 51 newcomers an opportunity to get a taste of playing in The Horseshoe before heading into the summer.And the scrimmage provided a chance to learn plenty about the new-look Buckeyes.From freshman receiver Chris Henry Jr. to new special teams coordinator Robby Discher, here’s what stood out from the 2026 Ohio State Spring Game. An Ohio State legend is on the verge of building something special by repeating history at the NFL Draft: Stephen MeansOhio State football could tie a 59-year-old NFL Draft record this week: Buckeye BreakfastOhio State linebacker Sonny Styles gets burrito named after himOhio State’s ‘Developed Here’ mantra still holds up in the transfer portal era. This stat shows whyChris Henry Jr. shines in first spring gameHenry lost his black stripe earlier this week, and he proved worthy of the honor Saturday.Henry caught the first long pass of the afternoon, a completion of approximately 35 yards from sophomore quarterback Tavien St. Clair. On his next drive, Henry caught a 40-yard touchdown pass from St. Clair.Three-star signee Brock Boyd earned plenty of buzz early in the spring, becoming the first freshman to shed his black stripe on March 31. But Henry, a former five-star prospect, is starting to earn headlines as he prepares to serve as Jeremiah Smith’s backup at X-receiver. How Ohio State handled absences in secondaryOhio State was without safety Earl Little Jr. and cornerback Jay Timmons for at least the last two practices of the spring — a pair of sessions open to the media. In their absence, Ohio State tweaked how it handled the nickelback position, or the “star” as the Buckeyes label it. Cornerback Jermaine Mathews typically moved inside when necessary, with Georgia transfer Dominick Kelly coming in to take his spot on the outside opposite of Devin Sanchez.When the Buckeyes had three tight ends on the field — Christian Alliegro at the line of scrimmage with Payton Pierce and TJ Alford at the second level — Kelly went to the sideline while Mathews and Sanchez remained the outside corners.Jaylen McClain, one of three returning starters on defense, was the strong safety while Terry Moore was the free safety with the first team. Moore, who transferred in from Duke this offseason, split first- and second-team reps with safety Leroy Roker this offseason. The spring game suggests Moore will enter the summer as the favorite to win that battle. Close eye on Ohio State’s special teamsThe early returns on Discher have been promising, and the spring game showed signs of progress.Joe McGuire booted a 47-yard punt in the first half, pinning the offense at its own three-yard line. Coach Ryan Day praised McGuire’s hangtime earlier this spring, but he noted a desire to see better yardage, with the Australian averaging 42.4 yards per punt in his career.The unit in front of McGuire also looks different with Discher putting an offensive lineman in the center of OSU’s shield. Gabe VanSickle, who’s been working as the first team right guard, often held the role.Ohio State has used shields in the past, but defensive end Caden Curry and linebacker Cody Simon were part of the equation in recent years. The spring game is a low-stakes competition, so simulating pressure for kicker Connor Hawkins is difficult. He passed his first test as a Buckeye inside Ohio Stadium, connecting on a 46-yard field goal in the first half. However, later in the half, he missed a 43-yard attempt in the rain.",
